// EVERY ENFORCED PRIVATE WITNESS, IN ONE PROCESS.
//
// This module exists because of a measurement, not a preference. The roster's CI step ran one
// `gunbc run` per witness: 33 completed invocations on run 34462653642, 82-107s each, mean 96s,
// spread under ten percent. Witnesses doing very different amounts of work cost the same, so what
// was being paid for was not witness work but the composed world -- resolved and typechecked from
// scratch, once per process, with both cache roots unset so nothing carried. Calling every
// witness's main from one entry pays that once. Nothing about the witnesses changes; they are
// imported and called exactly as CI called them.
//
// ONE WITNESS IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and it is the one a reader will look for. test.claim.
// projection_drift_witness is a declared PrivateWitness row but is NOT a member of
// private_witnesses, because it is emitted to its own workflow file: a verifier discovered by the
// artifact it verifies can be deleted by the same drift it exists to catch. It is therefore not
// Enforced by this roster's reckoning and does not belong here. The coverage conjunct below is what
// caught my first cut including it -- I had read the declared rows rather than the roster's own
// membership list, and the substrate disagreed with me correctly.
//
// THE COVERAGE CONJUNCT IS FIRST AND IS NOT DECORATION. This module and
// strategy.private_witness_workflow both name the enforced set -- unavoidably, because .dag has no
// reflection and a call has to be written to happen. Two lists of one fact is the fork DESIGN
// section 3 forbids, so they are checked against each other in both directions before any verdict
// is read: an Enforced row with no RosterOutcome here, or a RosterOutcome here that is not Enforced
// there, reds the run. A witness silently dropped from this list is exactly the failure that would
// otherwise be invisible -- the roster would still pass, over one fewer witness.
//
// WHAT ONE PROCESS COSTS, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Assertion failures keep their names: each
// witness's ProcessExit is folded and the exit reason lists every failing entry with its own reason.
// A RESOLVE failure cannot be folded at all -- a module that does not resolve cannot be called, so
// one broken import fails this entry as a whole. That was the objection to this shape, and it was
// tested by breaking a witness deliberately rather than reasoned about: the resolve diagnostic names
// the failing file, line, column and the module that imported it, identically to running that
// witness alone. Both diagnostics are quoted verbatim in the annotation above roster_aggregate_run_line in
// strategy.private_witness_workflow, which is where the measurement is re-derivable from.
type RosterOutcome {
  entry: NonEmptyStr
  exit: ProcessExit
}

